    {
      "body": "This fixes the spurious removal for consumers that keep a set of hashes, but `BlockStored` is still emitted for every copy, so consumers that count references would now keep hashes the pod has already evicted.\n\n1. `vllm/v1/core/block_pool.py:620`: removal is now per hash, but stores are still per copy (`cache_full_blocks`, `cache_partial_block`). A hash with two copies gives two stores and one removal, so a consumer that counts references never gets back to zero. llm-d-router counts this way (`pkg/kvevents/event_dedup_filter.go`), so it would keep routing to prefixes the pod no longer holds. Either emit `BlockStored` only for the first copy as well, or keep per-copy events and expose the remaining count, as #48933 does.\n\n2. #60616 documents `BlockRemoved` as per copy, and its `test_each_copy_of_a_hash_is_stored_and_removed` would fail with this change. The offloading connectors also stay per copy, so one stream would carry two meanings. Could the semantics be settled on #60616 first, and one of this PR, #55092 and #48933 picked?\n\n3. `tests/v1/core/test_prefix_caching.py:3351`: the duplicate is built with `set_block_hash` and `insert` directly, so the test never goes through `cache_full_blocks`. A test that caches two real copies and evicts them, asserting store and removal counts per hash, would cover the real path.\n",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/58508#pullrequestreview-5557283414",
